"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[2897],{62897:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"slug":"deficit-byudzheta-2026-7-trln-zaimy","locale":"en","title":"Finance Ministry: the 2026 budget deficit will reach 7.3 trillion rubles, almost 2 times the plan","description":"The Finance Ministry estimates a 2026 deficit of 7.3 trillion rubles, or 3.2% of GDP. Where the gap comes from, how much the state will borrow, and how it affects deposits and loans.","blocks":[{"id":"b001","kind":"paragraph","text":"The Finance Ministry estimates the federal budget deficit in 2026 at 7.3 trillion rubles, or 3.2% of GDP. The budget law had set it at 3.8 trillion, or 1.6% of GDP; in 2025 it was 5.7 trillion. Expenditure in 2026 will exceed the plan by 4.5 trillion rubles, reaching 48.6 trillion. Revenue will be roughly 1 trillion higher: non-oil-and-gas revenue adds 2.3 trillion, while oil-and-gas revenue falls 1.3 trillion to 7.6 trillion rubles. The resulting 3.5-trillion gap adds to the planned 3.8 trillion."},{"id":"b002","kind":"paragraph","text":"The ministry does not specify which expenditure increased. Defence-spending execution has been classified since 2022. Budget documents allocate 12.1 trillion rubles to defence in 2026 and 17.1 trillion in 2027. An unofficial estimate puts this year\'s total at around 17 trillion by year-end. If correct, defence exceeds plan by roughly 5 trillion, more than the entire budget overspend, leaving other categories below plan. From January to July 2026, expenditure grew 14.5% year on year and revenue 8.8%. The ministry then attributed the gap to advance contract payments."},{"id":"b003","kind":"paragraph","text":"The state will borrow most of the shortfall: net borrowing rises to 5 trillion rubles instead of the programme\'s 4.2 trillion. Another 459 billion rubles comes from the National Wealth Fund. Together that covers roughly 5.5 trillion of the 7.3 trillion. Ministry materials do not explain the remaining funding. For QIV, the ministry plans 2.45 trillion rubles of federal loan bonds, or OFZ, 63% above the QII and QIII plans. Year-end public debt rises to 46.1 trillion rubles, or 19.9% of GDP."},{"id":"b004","kind":"paragraph","text":"Imagine a town with one bank where a borrower suddenly needs an enormous sum. The bank has no additional money, so it raises rates and credit becomes dearer for everyone. In debt markets, that borrower is the state. This is crowding out: government borrowing takes funds that might have gone to businesses and households, raising their price. On September 23, 2026, the ministry sold 12-year OFZ at 16.44% annually while the Bank of Russia\'s key rate was 14%. Investors price in the possibility that inflation and rates remain high for a long time."},{"id":"b005","kind":"paragraph","text":"People feel this through rates. When the state borrows heavily and expensively, banks find OFZ attractive and are willing to pay more for deposits. For the same reason, loan rates fall more slowly. Spending beyond revenue adds demand and pushes prices upward. The Bank of Russia considers the budget when setting rates. Its next decision is on October 23."},{"id":"b006","kind":"paragraph","text":"A long deposit locks in today\'s rate, although accelerating inflation could consume part of the return. A short deposit leaves funds accessible, but banks quickly cut interest when rates fall. Credit is expensive now. Waiting for cheap credit is also a risk: while the budget borrows 2.45 trillion rubles a quarter, nobody promises lower rates."},{"id":"b007","kind":"heading","text":"What is Russia\'s budget deficit in 2026?"},{"id":"b008","kind":"paragraph","text":"The ministry estimates 7.3 trillion rubles, or 3.2% of GDP. The budget law had planned 3.8 trillion rubles, or 1.6% of GDP. In 2025 it was 5.7 trillion rubles."},{"id":"b009","kind":"heading","text":"How does the state cover its budget deficit?"},{"id":"b010","kind":"paragraph","text":"Mainly through borrowing: net OFZ borrowing in 2026 rises to 5 trillion rubles, with 2.45 trillion planned for QIV issuance. Another 459 billion rubles comes from the National Wealth Fund."},{"id":"b011","kind":"heading","text":"How does a budget deficit affect deposit and loan rates?"},{"id":"b012","kind":"paragraph","text":"Heavy state borrowing makes money dearer for everyone else. On September 23, 2026, the ministry sold 12-year OFZ at 16.44% annually against a 14% key rate. While banks can earn that yield on government debt, deposits stay attractive and loans become cheaper only slowly."}],"sources":[{"label":"Finance Ministry raises its deficit estimate for ’26 to 3.2% of GDP, plans Wealth Fund use and more borrowing","url":"https://ru.themoscowtimes.com/2026/09/29/eksklyuziv-minfin-povysil-otsenku-defitsita-byudzheta-rf-v-26g-do-32-vvp-potratit-chast-fnb-i-narastit-zaymy-a207226"},{"label":"Russian budget deficit rises to 6.455 trillion rubles","url":"https://www.profinance.ru/news2/2026/08/11/cjyj-defitsit-rossijskogo-byudzheta-vyros-do-6455-trilliona-rublej.html"},{"label":"Russia to increase military spending in ’27 by 27% against plan","url":"https://ru.themoscowtimes.com/2026/09/28/eksklyuziv-rf-uvelichit-voennye-raskhody-v-27g-na-27-ot-plana-a207209"},{"label":"Russian military spending this year to approach 17 trillion rubles, 40% above plan","url":"https://thebell.io/voennye-raskhody-rossii-v-etom-godu-vyrastut-pochti-do-17-trln-rubley-na-40-bolshe-plana"},{"label":"Finance Ministry plans 2.45 trillion rubles of OFZ issuance in QIV","url":"https://www.interfax.ru/russia/1119745"},{"label":"Finance Ministry places 15.3 billion rubles of OFZ PD-26253 at 16.44%","url":"https://ru.themoscowtimes.com/2026/09/23/minfin-rf-razmestil-ofz-pd-26253-na-153-mlrd-r-pod-1644-pri-sprose-665-mlrd-r-a206786"},{"label":"Bank of Russia keeps its key rate at 14%","url":"https://www.cbr.ru/press/keypr/"}],"sourceBodySha256":"0318e1238e0b1b917560ee37a35c2d4c75abd5d23ee16e401de880eeb401e7a6"}')}}]);